  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1128 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA strong upper low continues to spin over the eastern CONUS,\ncentered over central Tennessee. A surface low has developed in\nresponse and is located within the central Appalachian Mountains.\nEnough lift and moisture remains within the deformation zone\nalong the back side of the troughing to continue bringing light\nprecipitation into the Mid-South. So, light snow and rain will\ncontinue, primarily across West Tennessee, through tonight with\nimpacts gradually improving. Enough remaining snowfall is\nexpected to keep the Winter Weather Advisories across Western\nTennessee through Midnight.\n\nBoth the surface low and upper trough will move east with\nprecipitation and impacts leaving along with them through Friday.\nUpper level confluence and height rises will lead to an increase\nin surface pressure over the Gulf Coastline into Saturday.\nSurface winds will then turn southwesterly, bringing warmer\ntemperatures back into the forecast with highs returning to 50s\nand 60s this weekend. In the meantime, lows are expected to\nremain around 30 degrees throughout the region.\n\nThe upper pattern will become more zonal across the eastern CONUS\nthrough this upcoming weekend. A trough will then amplify over\nthe Mississippi River Valley through Sunday and into the\nbeginning of next week. A surface low will develop in response\nover the Red River Valley, tracking eastward into the Ohio River\nValley by Sunday morning. A cold front will accompany this system\nand will pass through the Mid-South in tandem. Low-level moisture\nadvection will occur in advance of the cold front, but the\ncurrent low will scour the Gulf of moisture. As a result,\natmospheric moisture will remain low, causing QPF to remain below\n0.50\" with this system. Enough mid-level cooling will be present\nto produce some MUCAPE (< 500 J/kg) with an attendant thunder\nrisk, but no severe weather is expected. The front will clear the\nregion by Sunday evening bringing cooler temperatures and high\npressure back into the region Monday.\n\nEnsembles bring zonal upper flow back across the CONUS into the\nmiddle of next week, quickly moderating temperatures behind\nSunday's frontal passage. By Wednesday, high temperatures are\nlikely to climb into the upper 60s and low 70s. Ensembles are\nrelatively in agreement that the broader upper pattern will then\ndevelop a trough over the Plains through Friday, pushing it east\nthrough the end of the period. So, as of right now, expect a more\nunsettled end to next week and the end of the forecast, but exact\ndetails remain out of reach in this forecast cycle.",
